<script setup lang="ts">
import { onMounted, onUnmounted, ref } from 'vue'
import stages from '../data/manual-journey.json'
import corpus from '../data/manual-corpus.json'
import ManualJourneyMap from './ManualJourneyMap.vue'
import ManualRanking from './ManualRanking.vue'
import ManualAttribution from './ManualAttribution.vue'
import ManualEvaluation from './ManualEvaluation.vue'
import './manual-journey.css'
const emit = defineEmits<{ ready: [] }>()
const currentStage = ref('question')
let frame: number | undefined
function trackStage() {
  if (frame !== undefined) return
  frame = window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    frame = undefined
    let current = stages[0]!.id
    for (const stage of stages) if ((document.getElementById(stage.id)?.getBoundingClientRect().top ?? Infinity) <= Math.min(180, window.innerHeight * .3)) current = stage.id
    if (window.scrollY > 0 && window.scrollY + window.innerHeight >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2) current = stages[stages.length - 1]!.id
    currentStage.value = current
  })
}
onMounted(() => { emit('ready'); window.addEventListener('scroll', trackStage, { passive: true }); window.addEventListener('resize', trackStage); trackStage() })
onUnmounted(() => { window.removeEventListener('scroll', trackStage); window.removeEventListener('resize', trackStage); if (frame !== undefined) window.cancelAnimationFrame(frame) })
</script>
<template>
  <section class="case-heading wrap"><a class="back" href="/projects/">All projects</a><p class="eyebrow">JavaScript · BM25-style retrieval · Exact source references</p><h1>Technical<br>Manual Search</h1><p class="hero-description">A useful search result should lead back to a passage you can inspect. Follow the corpus, ranking and evaluation behind a local search tool that keeps its misses and unsupported matches visible.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="#manual-build-map">Explore the build journey</a><a class="button secondary" href="/demos/technical-manual-search/">Open interactive demo</a></div><div class="case-facts"><div><span>Project</span><strong>Personal project · October 2026</strong></div><div><span>Corpus</span><strong>3 fictional manuals · 15 sections</strong></div><div><span>Recorded verification · 13 Oct 2026</span><strong>24 native tests · 17 browser checks</strong></div></div></section>
  <ManualJourneyMap />
  <section class="case-layout wrap section manual-journey"><nav class="case-nav manual-journey-nav" aria-label="Build journey"><div class="manual-nav-intro"><strong>Build journey</strong><span>Six stages, with evidence.</span></div><a v-for="(stage, position) in stages" :key="stage.id" :href="'#' + stage.id" :aria-current="currentStage === stage.id ? 'step' : undefined"><span class="manual-stage-number" aria-hidden="true">{{ String(position + 1).padStart(2, '0') }}</span><span>{{ stage.label }}</span></a></nav><div class="case-content">
    <section id="question" class="manual-stage"><p class="eyebrow">01 / Define attributable retrieval</p><h2>Show the passage behind the match.</h2><p>A reference search can find familiar words without finding an answer. The interface needs to expose the source, while the evaluation needs to retain both missed relevant sections and unsupported questions that return matches.</p><dl class="manual-brief"><div><dt>The goal</dt><dd>Search a small, inspectable set of fictional manuals and make every result attributable to an exact section and revision.</dd></div><div><dt>The boundary</dt><dd>Lexical retrieval with no generated answers, external service or semantic model. Browser and CLI use the same pure ranking module.</dd></div><div><dt>The evidence</dt><dd>Keep per-query labels, returned references and failures alongside aggregate metrics. Do not treat a ranking score as answer confidence.</dd></div></dl><p class="manual-handoff">Before ranking, define a corpus whose identity, revisions and source wording can be checked.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-development.md">Read the original development log</a></section>
    <section id="corpus" class="manual-stage"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Build a bounded corpus</p><h2>Stable sections. Original wording.</h2><p>Three project-authored training manuals provide five sections each. Validation rejects duplicate identities and invalid fields. Indexing copies source text exactly while keeping normalised term frequencies private.</p><div class="manual-library"><article v-for="(item, position) in corpus" :key="item.id"><svg viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 6h30v36H8z M14 6v36 M20 15h12 M20 23h12 M20 31h8" /></svg><span>0{{ position + 1 }} / {{ item.category }}</span><h3>{{ item.title }}</h3><p>Revision {{ item.revision }} · {{ item.sections.length }} sections</p></article></div><details class="manual-disclosure"><summary>Inspect the identity and validation contract</summary><pre>{
  "id": "cedar-logger",
  "revision": "1.2",
  "sections": [{
    "id": "csv-export",
    "title": "Export session data as CSV",
    "text": "Exact original paragraph…"
  }]
}</pre><p class="metadata">Abbreviated shape; a complete manual also requires a title and category. Canonical reference: <code>cedar-logger#csv-export</code>. IDs contain lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, beginning with a letter. Manual IDs are globally unique; section IDs are unique within a manual.</p></details><p>Original text is used for display and attribution. The index combines the manual title once, section heading twice and paragraph once; repeating the heading is an explicit weighting choice, not a learned parameter.</p><p class="manual-handoff">Make tokenisation and ranking shared code, so the browser, CLI and evaluator retrieve the same sections.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-architecture.md">Read the corpus and indexing architecture</a></section>
    <section id="ranking" class="manual-stage"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Implement shared ranking</p><h2>Trace the words. Inspect the order.</h2><p>The core applies Unicode NFKC normalisation, lowercase and stopword removal. Query terms are deduplicated; any one may produce a lexical match. Manual and category filters intersect while document statistics remain global.</p><ManualRanking /><details class="manual-disclosure"><summary>See the scoring rule and limits</summary><pre>idf = log(1 + (N - df + 0.5) / (df + 0.5))
score += idf × tf × (k₁ + 1)
         / (tf + k₁ × (1 - b + b × length / averageLength))
k₁ = 1.2; b = 0.75</pre><p class="metadata">N, document frequency and average section length use the entire corpus. Ties use stable reference ordering. Queries over 240 UTF-16 code units or 64 distinct non-stopword terms are rejected. There is no stemming, synonym expansion, spelling correction or tuned minimum-score threshold.</p></details><p class="manual-handoff">Ranking gives an order, not an answer. Preserve enough source information for the reader to judge relevance.</p></section>
    <section id="sources" class="manual-stage"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Keep the source reachable</p><h2>A citation should open the actual paragraph.</h2><p>Each result includes the manual revision, exact section reference, matched-term fraction, excerpt and complete original text. The standalone demo keeps query and filters in the URL and restores them on back, forward and reload.</p><ManualAttribution /><p class="manual-handoff">Source visibility makes an individual result inspectable. Labelled queries test whether retrieval finds the intended sections consistently.</p></section>
    <section id="evaluation" class="manual-stage"><p class="eyebrow">05 / Measure misses and false matches</p><h2>One denominator cannot tell both stories.</h2><p>The recorded set has 18 positive queries and 8 negative or unsupported queries. Every positive, including a miss, contributes to MRR, recall@5 and hit@5. Negative lexical matches are reported separately.</p><ManualEvaluation /><div class="manual-failures"><article><span>Retained miss / p17</span><h3>The right vocabulary is missing from the intended section.</h3><p>“Download readings” returns the missing-samples section through “readings”, but misses the labelled CSV export section. All three positive metrics count it as zero.</p></article><article><span>Retained false matches / n05–n08</span><h3>Shared words still match unsupported questions.</h3><p>Warranty, encryption, load-rating and price questions produce lexical matches without supporting passages. All four remain in the negative false-positive rate.</p></article></div><div class="stats"><div><strong>24</strong><span>recorded native tests</span></div><div><strong>17</strong><span>recorded browser checks</span></div><div><strong>26</strong><span>retained labelled queries</span></div></div><p>Verified on 13 October 2026, 10:10 (UTC+8), for v1.1.0 using Node 24.19.0 and desktop Chromium 154.0.8037.98. Checks cover ranking, source fidelity, URL state, real CLI behavior, safe rendering and 360 / 390 / 768 / 1440 px layouts.</p><p>The final verification evaluated the same inputs twice and confirmed byte-identical JSON. A boundary-test fixture was also corrected: one of 65 generated terms was a stopword, leaving only 64 searchable terms. The fix changed the test data, not the ranking.</p><div class="actions"><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-evaluation.json" download>Download every query outcome</a><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-verification.json">Inspect the dated verification</a></div></section>
    <section id="reproduce" class="manual-stage"><p class="eyebrow">06 / Package reproducibility</p><h2>Keep the corpus, labels and misses together.</h2><p>The source archive includes all three manuals, query judgements, per-query output, corpus and label hashes, tests and development notes. Evaluation output has no generated timestamp, making repeated runs directly comparable.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/demos/technical-manual-search/">Open interactive demo</a><a class="button secondary" href="/downloads/technical-manual-search.zip" download>Download source and tests</a></div><h3>Run the source locally</h3><pre>node --test tests/*.test.mjs
node scripts/search.mjs "battery replacement" --json
node scripts/evaluate.mjs
node scripts/serve.mjs</pre><p>Use Node 24 or later from the extracted directory. No install or build step is needed. Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:4177/demos/technical-manual-search/</code>. A valid CLI search exits 0 even with no matches; invalid input exits 2. Browser verification uses the separately documented Playwright setup.</p><div class="manual-files"><a href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-readme.md"><strong>README</strong><span>Run, search and verify</span></a><a href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-queries.json" download><strong>Query labels</strong><span>All 26 judgements and references</span></a><a href="/evidence/technical-manual-search-evaluation.json" download><strong>Evaluation</strong><span>Metrics, hashes and every result</span></a></div><h3>Scope and contribution</h3><p>The project covers the retrieval core, fictional corpus, source interface, URL state and reproducible evaluation. These six stages group the recorded development work; they are not individual commits.</p><p>The corpus and relevance judgements are small and project-authored. The measurements do not establish performance on real manuals or native phones. There is no generated answer, semantic search, remote backend or offline cache. Fictional training passages are not instructions for real equipment.</p></section>
  </div></section>
</template>
